**How to implement.** The residual closures, the Jacobian adapter and the generic scaling move into `MuellerFitting` (001 §Q AT-2 '`observeWith`, `residualFor`, `throughputResidualFor`; `scalingAround`'; the S10.1 brief). This is a MOVE (.spec-md §4 'A reuse is a move or a call: the new home is created and the old body deleted in the same step'; .spec-md §7 'Harness and samples … Migration provided: a move'): after the step exactly one declaration of each moved name exists. (1) Into `MuellerFitting.fs` (step 335), after `observeWith`, with their signatures and bodies unchanged: `residualFor` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:residualFor`), the total, fixed-length 15-per-observation closure with its 1.0e3 penalty; `throughputResidualFor` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:throughputResidualFor`), 16 per observation, the 16th the log-throughput difference; `residualJacobianAt` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:residualJacobianAt`), the `FitQuality.residualJacobian` adapter over `ScaledVector`; and `scalingAround` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:scalingAround`). Every signature already speaks `ForwardModelProxy`, `MuellerObservation`, `ParameterScaling`, `ScaledVector`, `ResidualVector` and `ResidualJacobian`, the last three `[<NumericCarrier>]` (`MuellerInverse.fs:ResidualVector`, `:ScaledVector`, `:ResidualJacobian`), so no shape changes. Each moved declaration's doc is cut to its contract in one to three lines (AGENTS.md 'Comments: short, precise, useful'), keeping one line where it guards a hazard the code cannot show. The throughput closure keeps one line saying the log term restores the isotropic absorption that normalization discards (specs/0044/.manual/018-absorbing-biaxial-inverse.md). (2) `InverseFitHarness.fs` deletes the four; its `fitWithResidual`, `fitObservations` and `fitAbsorbingObservations`, which step 337 moves, call the product closures. Every consumer opens the product module where the compiler asks and changes nothing else unless named here. The generic `scalingAround` has no caller in BerremanTests today: `BiaxialSample.scalingAround`, `AbsorbingBiaxialSample.absorbingScalingAround` and `MuellerInverseTests.scalingAround` build their own one-argument specialisations and shadow it. It moves because 001 §Q AT-2 lists it for the study engine (S11). The shadowing comment above `MuellerInverseTests.scalingAround` ('It deliberately shadows the harness's general `scalingAround`') names `MuellerFitting.scalingAround`. BerremanTests' numbers are bit-identical (.spec-md §4 'BerremanTests' numbers stay unchanged'): the bodies are unchanged. No test is added or removed. `FeasibleBiaxialInverseTests` reaches `residualFor` and `residualJacobianAt`, and `AbsorbingBiaxialInverseTests` reaches `throughputResidualFor`, the two cheapest classes that do.

**Acceptance.** After the move, `MuellerFitting.fs` MUST declare `residualFor`, `throughputResidualFor`, `residualJacobianAt` and `scalingAround` with unchanged signatures and bodies, and `InverseFitHarness.fs` MUST declare none of them; unit-tests MUST pass over the selected classes, `BerremanTests.FeasibleBiaxialInverseTests` and `BerremanTests.AbsorbingBiaxialInverseTests`, their numbers and counts unchanged; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, its count unchanged; and build, elevation and the nineteen s33 invariants MUST pass.
